Now I have all the context needed. Here's your complete MCP server setup:

---

**Program.cs** — stdio transport with DI:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("MyApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

**ApiTools.cs** — tool class wrapping a REST API:

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetch a resource by its unique identifier.")]
    public async Task<string> GetResource(
        [Description("The unique resource ID")] string id,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching resource {Id}", id);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("MyApi");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/resources/{id}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search resources by keyword query.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchResources(
        [Description("Search keyword")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("MyApi");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/resources?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = false, Destructive = false),
     Description("Create a new resource with a name and description.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateResource(
        [Description("Resource name")] string name,
        [Description("Resource description")] string description,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("MyApi");
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, description });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await client.PostAsync("/resources", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>` are injected via the primary constructor. The class is resolved from the DI container per invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters. The SDK distinguishes them automatically. |

**Key rule:** Parameters that are DI services (`HttpClient`, `ILogger`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<>`) are **never** exposed in the tool's JSON schema. Only parameters with `[Description]` appear to the LLM.

In the example above, `IHttpClientFactory` and `ILogger<ApiTools>` are constructor-injected because `ApiTools` is a non-static class. They're registered in `Program.cs` via `AddHttpClient()` (logging is registered by default). The SDK discovers the class via `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` and resolves it from DI on each tool call.
